616 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (The literature on automated evaluation systems highlights the growing importance of Artificial Intelligence in transforming traditional educational practices. Early approaches to answer evaluation primarily focused on objective-type questions using rule-based systems and simple pattern matching techniques. While these methods were effective for multiple-choice questions, they lacked the ability to evaluate descriptive answers and could not handle handwritten content. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [3]
>>
endobj
617 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (With the advancement of image processing and machine learning techniques, Optical Character Recognition \(OCR\) systems were introduced to convert handwritten or printed text into machine-readable format. Initial OCR systems were mainly designed for printed text and struggled with handwritten inputs due to variations in writing styles, noise, and image quality. This limitation restricted their application in real-world educational scenarios. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [4]
>>
endobj
618 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 229 0 R
/E (Recent developments in deep learning have significantly improved handwriting recognition capabilities. Convolutional Neural Networks \(CNNs\) and Recurrent Neural Networks \(RNNs\) have been widely used for recognizing handwritten text with higher accuracy. These models can learn complex patterns in handwriting and adapt to different writing styles, making them suitable for automated evaluation systems. )
/Pg 23 0 R
/K [5]
>>
endobj
